 >> We use to have CVCS(Centralized Version Control System) for multiple SD's to work on a project in parallel.
              Disadvantage: If the centralized server is down BOOM… and we don’t have version history in our local 

>> DVCS(distributed Version Control System):  here even if the server goes down we have entire commit history in the local 


Aspect	Centralized VCS (e.g., SVN)	Distributed VCS (e.g., Git)
Repository Location	Central server only	Every user has a full copy
Offline Work	Limited	Fully supported
Collaboration	Must push/pull via server	Can share directly (P2P)
Speed	Slower (network required)	Faster (local access)
Backup	Single point of failure	Multiple full backups



	• Centralized VCS = One master book in a library. You must go to the library to read or edit it.
	• Distributed VCS = Every student gets their own copy of the book. They can read and annotate anytime and share notes later.



