>> Git Init
	
   In git we have a working directory for the project we are working on and a staging directory. Git will track only the files which are in staging directory.
   We have to push the files into staging area and we have commit history when commit these files
  
  git status : This command will tell if the working directory had a local repository
		
  git init :  In order to create a local repository for the working directory we need to run git init command on local repository  
                  It creates a hidden folder /.git in the directory 
                  The files in the git are not tracked until and unless we add it to the staging area 
                  By default the name of the branch will be master
		  git init -b main : To make the branch as master
  rm -rf .git : To remove git from working repository 

		
		
>> Git Commit
       git add <filename> : To add the file to staging area
       git add . : To add all the files to staging area
       git commit -m <message>: To commit the files in staging area to remote repository 
                     Every time you commit some data to git even with small change in the file git creates a new checksum out of it with SHA1 
       git log: To give the commit history 
       git commit -a -m <message> : To commit changes directly from working directory to remote repository skipping staging, this file won't be tracked further since it is skipping staging

>> Git diff     
     git diff : It gives the changes between the working directory file and the file that is in staging area
     git diff --staged : It the file already in stating area and you want to track the latest changes then we use it 


>> Git remove
             To delete a file which is committed  
                    1) git rm --cached <filename> : To remove the file from git then
                    2)  Delete the file from working directory 
                    3)  Commit the changes
     
       
      

  

